{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ve reviewed your chart and the reason you came in. You had severe abdominal pain starting early yesterday morning, is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it woke me up at 3 AM. It was like 20 out of 10—way worse than my usual pain. Left side and upper belly. I tried a little food, but it made me nauseous and I couldn’t keep anything down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you’ve had these partial bowel obstructions before, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, three times this year already. I’ve had so many surgeries—bowel resections, adhesions cut, the whole thing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did an abdominal CT this afternoon. It shows a partial small bowel obstruction again, likely from adhesions. But the good news is, contrast made it into your colon, so it’s not a complete blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. So no surgery this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. We’re going to treat this conservatively—keep you NPO, give IV fluids, and let your gastric tube vent to decompress your stomach. We’ll monitor your pain and bowel function closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been off TPN for months now and gaining weight—up to 176. I was doing so well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2190-05-14 16:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great progress. We’ll work to get you back to eating normally. For now, rest and decompression are key."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2190-05-15 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling? Any change in pain or nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2190-05-15 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still nauseous. The tube helps a little, but I feel bloated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2190-05-15 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs are stable—no signs of infection or dehydration. We’re giving potassium and magnesium as needed. We’ve started hydromorphone for pain through a PCA pump so you can control it safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2190-05-15 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had bad reactions to some pain meds before. I’m allergic to a lot—fentanyl, oxycontin, meperidine…"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2190-05-15 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ve noted your allergies. Hydromorphone is different, and we’re monitoring you closely. If it causes issues, we’ll adjust."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2190-05-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your abdominal X-ray yesterday showed some improvement in distention. Still, we’re seeing a lot of fluid output from your G-tube."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2190-05-16 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel foggy today. And my speech is worse—I keep stumbling over words."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2190-05-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I noticed that. You’ve had aphasia before, right? We’re going to consult neurology to make sure this isn’t something new."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2190-05-16 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It comes and goes. Sometimes when I’m distracted, it gets better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2190-05-17 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi. The neurology team saw you. They think your aphasia and drowsiness are likely related to your chronic condition and possibly the pain meds, not a new stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2190-05-17 10:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2190-05-17 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They don’t think so. Your CT head was clear—no bleeding or new stroke. But we’re switching your pain med from hydromorphone to morphine because the PCA may have worsened your nausea and mental fog."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2190-05-17 10:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. I’ve been so tired."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2190-05-17 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Another X-ray this morning shows your large bowel is more dilated. We’re concerned about ileus—slowed bowel function—possibly from the narcotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2190-05-17 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the pain meds are making my gut slower?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2190-05-17 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ve stopped the PCA and are giving morphine only as needed. We’ll keep you NPO a bit longer and encourage walking to stimulate your bowels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2190-05-18 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you passed gas last night, and your G-tube output has decreased."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2190-05-18 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel a little better. Can I try some food?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2190-05-18 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start with ice chips and see how you do. If no nausea, we’ll advance to clear liquids tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2190-05-19 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You tolerated the clear liquids well. We’re going to try a soft diet today—applesauce, broth, mashed potatoes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2190-05-19 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. And my speech is clearer today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2190-05-19 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and you’re more alert. We’re also capping your G-tube intermittently to see if you can manage without constant venting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2190-05-20 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You had a small bowel movement this morning—that’s a great sign."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2190-05-20 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I still get nauseous if the tube’s capped too long."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2190-05-20 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "But it resolves after a few hours once you uncapped it. That’s expected. Your gut is still recovering."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2190-05-21 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did another CT head and abdominal X-ray today. The head scan was normal—no new issues. The abdominal film shows the obstruction is slightly improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2190-05-21 09:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "There was something about a metal object in the report?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2190-05-21 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. It’s the clip from your G-tube. It detached and is now floating in your bowel. It’s been seen before on old scans and has moved down. It’s not causing a blockage, so we’re just monitoring it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2190-05-21 09:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Shouldn’t it come out?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2190-05-21 09:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It likely will pass on its own. We’ll watch for any symptoms, but no intervention is needed now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2190-05-22 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been on a regular diet since yesterday and doing well. No vomiting, pain is at your baseline, and you’re walking regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2190-05-22 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m ready to go home. I miss my routine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2190-05-22 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning for discharge tomorrow. We’ll make sure you’re stable on oral meds and your enoxaparin is set for home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2190-05-23 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Last night’s vitals were stable—heart rate 112, oxygen 90% on room air. A bit low on oxygen, but you’re not short of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2190-05-23 07:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve always had a fast heart rate—usually in the 120s. And I’ve had asthma, but it’s mild."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2190-05-23 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your history notes chronic tachycardia and asthma. As long as you’re not struggling to breathe, we’re okay to proceed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2190-05-23 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve scheduled your follow-up with surgery in two weeks. You’ll keep taking all your home meds, including gabapentin, lamotrigine, and rabeprazole."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2190-05-23 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the enoxaparin for my blood thinning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2190-05-23 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue that daily. You’ve had DVTs before, so it’s important to prevent clots."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2190-05-24 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more thing—your discharge instructions say to eat soft foods for a few weeks and drink plenty of fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2190-05-24 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And walk a lot to keep my bowels moving?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2190-05-24 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And call if you develop fever, severe pain, vomiting, or can’t eat or drink."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2190-05-24 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re finalizing your discharge papers. You’re doing well—eating regular food, pain controlled, and moving independently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2190-05-24 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad this episode didn’t set me back too much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2190-05-24 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made great progress. Just take it slow at home and follow up as planned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2190-05-25 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your final vitals are stable. We’ll discharge you this afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2190-05-25 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2190-05-25 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Nurse will review your meds and home care. Remember—soft diet, walk daily, and monitor for warning signs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2190-05-25 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And keep my G-tube capped unless I’m nauseous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2190-05-25 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and uncapping it should help symptoms. If nausea persists, call your surgeon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2190-05-25 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Discharge orders are in. You’ll go home with visiting nurse services to check in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2190-05-25 12:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s reassuring. I’ll follow up with my doctors."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2190-05-25 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check—your pain is manageable, you’re alert, and you’ve had normal bowel movements."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2190-05-25 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, back to my usual baseline."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2190-05-25 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. We’ll see you in clinic, but call sooner if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2190-05-25 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your ride is here. Take care, and keep up the good work."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2190-05-25 16:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I appreciate everything."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2190-05-14 16:35:00",
    "admission_end": "2190-05-25 17:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a complex history of sclerosing mesenteritis, chronic gastrointestinal dysmotility, and multiple prior bowel surgeries was admitted for acute abdominal pain, nausea, and inability to tolerate oral intake. Imaging confirmed a partial small bowel obstruction, managed conservatively with NPO status, intravenous hydration, and gastrointestinal decompression via her G-tube. She initially received hydromorphone via PCA for pain, but this was changed to as-needed morphine due to concerns for narcotic-induced ileus and worsening aphasia and somnolence. Neurology consultation attributed her neurological symptoms to psychiatric and chronic factors, as they improved with distraction and resolved with medication adjustment. Her bowel function gradually improved, with return of flatus and bowel movements, allowing advancement to a regular diet. A detached G-tube clip was noted to be freely migrating in the bowel but was not causing obstruction. She remained hemodynamically stable throughout, with no signs of infection or bleeding. By discharge, she was tolerating oral intake, ambulating independently, and back to her baseline pain and function. She was discharged home with follow-up plans, instructions to continue home medications including enoxaparin, and guidance on diet, activity, and warning signs.",
    "problems": [
      "Partial intestinal obstruction, unspecified as to cause",
      "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
      "Aphasia",
      "Sjogren syndrome, unspecified",
      "Other artificial openings of gastrointestinal tract status",
      "Other specified functional intestinal disorders",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Ileus, unspecified",
      "Adverse effect of other opioids, initial encounter",
      "Unspecified mood [affective] disorder",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Anxiety disorder, unspecified",
      "Vitamin D deficiency, unspecified",
      "Insomnia, unspecified",
      "Restlessness and agitation",
      "Somnolence",
      "Weakness",
      "Fluency disorder following cerebral infarction",
      "Long term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Personal history of other venous thrombosis and embolism"
    ]
  }
}
